  "summary": "Four of the five top-ranked documents directly apply graph neural network methods (specifically message passing over relational graphs) to hate speech detection. [V0097] (El-Sayed et al., 2022) evaluates on ToxiBench and reports AUROC of 0.93, the strongest result among the GNN-based studies. [V0099] (Gupta et al., 2024) applies the same GNN approach to the ProtMap dataset, achieving accuracy of 0.72. [V0100] (Haddad et al., 2025) applies message passing over relational graphs to the FireScope dataset, reporting a test MAE of 0.79. [V0098] (Fischer et al., 2023) also uses message passing over relational graphs for hate speech detection on GridWatch, obtaining F1 of 0.65. The fifth slot is filled by [V0112] (Chen et al., 2019), which addresses hate speech detection on FireScope with boosted decision-tree ensembles (accuracy 0.77); while not a GNN method, it is the next closest match on topic. Documents applying GNN methods to unrelated tasks (wildfire mapping, electricity prediction, etc.) are excluded as they do not satisfy the joint method+topic criterion.",
